    "SKILL.md": "---\nname: self-skill-learner\ndescription: Learn and improve skills through iterative evaluation loops, reflection, and targeted rewrites. Use whenever the user wants a skill to get better over time with measurable progress.\nlicense: Complete terms in LICENSE.txt\n---\n\n# Self-Skill Learner\n\nA skill for improving other skills (including itself) using repeatable feedback loops.\n\n## Mission\n\nTurn weak or inconsistent skills into reliable, reusable skills by iterating on:\n- trigger quality,\n- instruction clarity,\n- output quality,\n- and success rate across realistic prompts.\n\n## Timeout Guard\n\n- Run one focused improvement pass per turn.\n- Prefer minimal edits over full rewrites.\n- Evaluate with a small representative prompt set only.\n- Record only high-signal learning for the next pass.\n- Finish within a single concise report.\n\n## Common Operating Principles\n\n- Read only the docs/context required to execute correctly before acting.\n- Follow the user instruction exactly; avoid paraphrase and unrequested improvements.\n- Keep scope surgical: change only what the request names.\n- Verify outcomes using available checks/tests; do not rely on intuition.\n- Keep communication concise and direct.\n- Prefer fewer, bundled tool invocations over fragmented calls.\n- If a persistent note surface exists, leave a short generalized note for future runs.\n\n## What To Improve\n\n### 1) Triggering Quality\n- Make description explicit about when to use the skill.\n- Include multiple natural-language trigger cues.\n- Avoid vague wording that causes under-triggering.\n\n### 2) Instruction Quality\n- Remove duplicated or conflicting rules.\n- Prefer clear rationale over rigid, brittle prescriptions.\n- Ensure steps are actionable and ordered.\n\n### 3) Output Quality\n- Define expected output structure when useful.\n- Ensure outputs are useful to the user's likely audience.\n- Emphasize factual grounding and explicit uncertainty.\n\n### 4) Reliability\n- Stress test with varied prompt styles (short, long, messy, casual).\n- Ensure behavior remains stable across similar prompts.\n- Reduce reliance on fragile wording hacks.\n\n## Stop Criteria\n\nStop when one of these is true:\n- targets are met for two consecutive iterations,\n- improvements become marginal,\n- or user approves final version.\n\n## Guardrails\n\n- Do not fabricate evaluation outcomes.\n- Do not claim improvements without evidence.\n- Do not optimize only for benchmark prompts (avoid overfitting).\n- Do not broaden triggers so far that unrelated tasks invoke the skill."
